    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, CURRENT] Reviewed 2026-11-01 at dev 100b6fbb6e. entrypoints.py still clamps the connectivity boost with min(1.0, ...) and sorts by (rank_score, _language_weight, _effective_out_degree); _diversity_cap is unchanged. Fixture (80 Flask routes + an Express scanner/server.js with POST /scan, GET /health): 50 entrypoints are kept, 48 http_route all at rank 1.0 and all Python; both Express routes are dropped (alone they rank 1.0); the sketch HTTP Routes section lists 48 app.py routes and no Express route. Correction: the tie-break is not pure insertion order; the second key, language weight, is what pushes the minority-language service out. (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nFound while fixing INV-liraj. On thc after the fix, 291 of 372 http_route entrypoints tie at rank_score 1.0 (base 0.90/0.95 + connectivity boost, capped at 1.0), so their order is insertion order. Two consequences: (1) the entrypoint cap (495; _diversity_cap gives http_route 198 + overflow) keeps 240 routes and drops the Express service's POST /scan (rank 1.0) and GET /health (0.989) from scanner/server.js, which were the only routes listed before the fix -- a second service's routes are crowded out by an arbitrary tie order; (2) the sketch's HTTP Routes section shows the first 19 routes in router order (17 of 19 are LIVE routes).\n\nProposal to evaluate: a tie-break that spreads the cap/sketch selection across files/frameworks (round-robin by route file) and/or avoids boost saturation for routes. Measure on thc and one multi-service repo."  # 3efb
